<!--
  stander.xml — a planar ankle-balancer standing on a flat foot, written for Synapsa Commons
  lesson F15-L04 (standing balance: the centre of mass and the support polygon).

  Authored for this lesson by the Synapsa Commons project and released under CC0-1.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/). Nothing is downloaded: the file
  ships inside the lesson directory and the lesson never touches the network.

  WHY THIS MODEL

  The lesson's subject is the support polygon — the region of ground the machine can put
  pressure on — and what happens when the centre of mass leaves it. That requires a real
  FOOT with real contacts, which is exactly what F15-L08's pinned-ankle balancer does not
  have. So this model keeps contacts on and spends its simplicity elsewhere: one leg, one
  actuated joint, and motion confined to the x-z plane.

  GEOMETRY

  The foot is a box resting on a plane. It is attached to the world by three joints, which
  together give it the freedom of a planar rigid body - it can slide, lift and rotate, so
  it can tip about its toe or heel, skid, or leave the ground entirely. Nothing pins it.

      slide_x   foot translates along x        [m]
      slide_z   foot translates along z        [m]
      pitch     foot rotates about y           [rad]
      ankle     torso rotates relative to foot [rad]   <- the only ACTUATED joint

  Positive angles pitch the machine FORWARD (+x). Upright is qpos = [0, 0, 0, 0].

  THE FOOT IS DELIBERATELY NOT CENTRED ON THE ANKLE

  The box is 0.24 m long and its centre sits 0.04 m AHEAD of the ankle, so the footprint
  runs from -0.08 m (heel) to +0.16 m (toe) in the foot's own frame. A human foot is
  asymmetric in exactly this way, and here it is load-bearing pedagogy rather than
  decoration: the toe margin is about twice the heel margin, so the machine should survive
  a forward shove about twice as large as the backward one. The notebook measures both and
  compares the ratio against the ratio of the margins. If the support polygon were a
  metaphor rather than a mechanism, those two numbers would not agree.

  THE TORSO DOES NOT COLLIDE

  torso_geom carries contype="0" conaffinity="0", so the only thing that can touch the
  floor is the foot. That makes the support polygon exactly the foot's footprint and
  nothing else, which is what lets the notebook compute it from the foot geom alone. It
  also means a fallen machine passes through the floor instead of landing on it: a fall
  here is detected from STATE, never from the torso hitting something. The first draft of
  this model omitted the flag, and the capsule's lower cap - which reaches 0.01 m below the
  ankle - rested on the plane and held the whole machine up by its chest. The symptom was
  a single contact point carrying full body weight while the foot floated. Saying so here
  is cheaper than letting the next reader rediscover it.

  THE TORQUE LIMIT IS NOT THE REAL LIMIT, AND THAT IS THE LESSON

  ctrlrange is +-60 N*m, which sounds generous, and is. The machine weighs about 19.5 kg,
  so it presses on the floor with about 191 N. Ankle torque balances against ground
  pressure, and the pressure can only act inside the footprint, so the centre of pressure
  can be moved at most 0.083 m heelward or 0.157 m toeward of where the mass sits - worth
  about 16 N*m and 30 N*m respectively. Ask for more than that and the foot rotates off the
  ground rather than the body rotating upright: the motor obeys, the floor does not.

  The notebook measures that ceiling rather than asserting it, by sweeping a constant ankle
  torque and watching where the measured centre of pressure stops moving. The gap between
  the 60 N*m the motor offers and the roughly 16 N*m the foot will accept is the whole
  point of the section.

  CALIBRATION

  timestep 0.002 s with implicitfast: a flat box on a plane settles onto four corner
  contacts and stays there, and a step costs a few microseconds, so a full push ladder is
  seconds of wall clock rather than minutes. Masses are stated explicitly rather than
  derived from a density, so every number in the lesson's output traces back to something
  legible in this file.
-->
<mujoco model="f15l04_stander">
  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true"/>

  <option timestep="0.002" integrator="implicitfast" gravity="0 0 -9.81"/>

  <default>
    <geom friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.008 1" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001"/>
  </default>

  <visual>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global elevation="-15" azimuth="90"/>
  </visual>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 3" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="5 5 0.1" pos="0 0 0" rgba="0.26 0.28 0.33 1"/>

    <body name="foot" pos="0 0 0.03">
      <joint name="slide_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0"/>
      <joint name="slide_z" type="slide" axis="0 0 1"/>
      <joint name="pitch"   type="hinge" axis="0 1 0"/>
      <!-- Box centre 0.04 m ahead of the ankle: footprint -0.08 (heel) .. +0.16 (toe). -->
      <geom name="foot_geom" type="box" size="0.12 0.05 0.03" pos="0.04 0 0" mass="1.5"
            rgba="0.55 0.58 0.66 1"/>

      <body name="torso" pos="0 0 0.03">
        <joint name="ankle" type="hinge" axis="0 1 0" range="-0.8 0.8" damping="1.0"/>
        <!-- contype/conaffinity 0: the torso cannot touch anything, so the support polygon
             is exactly the footprint and a fall is read from state, not from a collision. -->
        <geom name="torso_geom" type="capsule" fromto="0 0 0 0 0 0.95" size="0.07" mass="18"
              contype="0" conaffinity="0" rgba="0.72 0.74 0.80 1"/>
        <site name="head" pos="0 0 0.95" size="0.02"/>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <actuator>
    <!-- A direct torque motor: data.ctrl[0] is a TORQUE IN NEWTON-METRES, not a target
         angle. The limit is far above what the foot can accept; see the note above. -->
    <motor name="ankle_torque" joint="ankle" gear="1" ctrllimited="true" ctrlrange="-60 60"/>
  </actuator>

  <keyframe>
    <!-- Upright and at rest. The disturbance in this lesson is not a starting pose, as it
         was in F15-L08; it is a push applied through xfrc_applied part-way through the
         episode, so the machine has to be standing calmly before it is hit. -->
    <key name="stand" qpos="0 0 0 0" qvel="0 0 0 0"/>
  </keyframe>
</mujoco>
